## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical webhook security testing skills at a beginner level, focusing on cryptographic verification and attack scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- HMAC verification
- Timing-safe comparisons
- Timestamp tolerance
- Replay protection
- Test harness usage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js example
const crypto = require('crypto');
function sign(body, secret){
  return crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(body).digest('hex');
}
function verify(body, ts, provided, secret){
  const expected = sign(body, secret);
  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(provided,'hex'), Buffer.from(expected,'hex')) && Math.abs(Date.now() - Number(ts)) < 300000;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate replay protection in a distributed environment?
- What changes if the webhook payload is large or binary?

## Why This Is Asked
Asynchronous order processing with idempotency is common in production; testing must prove duplicates don't create multiple orders and failures route to DLQs with retries, plus end-to-end visibility.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys, exactly-once processing
- Message queues (Kafka), DLQ design
- Retry/backoff strategies, consumer failure modes
- End-to-end tracing and correlation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test (pseudo)
const request = require('supertest');
const api = require('supertest')('https://api.example.com');
const idKey = 'order-uniq-123';

test('duplicate submissions do not create duplicates', async () => {
  const r1 = await api.post('/orders').set('Idempotency-Key', idKey).send({items:[{sku:'A1',qty:1}]});
  const r2 = await api.post('/orders').set('Idempotency-Key', idKey).send({items:[{sku:'A1',qty:1}]});
  expect(r1.status).toBe(202);
  expect(r2.status).toBe(202);
  // fetch status after processing
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate DLQ contents and retry semantics across multiple partitions?
- What observability would you add to prove end-to-end visibility across services?

Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of idempotent APIs, distributed write reliability, and multi-region read-model consistency under failure.

Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys and deduplication windows
- Outbox/CDC patterns for exactly-once processing
- Cross-region latency and eventual consistency
- Schema evolution and backward compatibility
- Failure modes: primary failover, network partitions, back-pressure

Code Example
```javascript
// Example: idempotent POST /orders with dedupe check
async function submitOrder(key, payload) {
  const exists = await redis.get(`order_idem:${key}`);
  if (exists) return JSON.parse(exists);
  const order = await db.createOrder(payload);
  await redis.set(`order_idem:${key}`, JSON.stringify(order), 'EX', 3600);
  await outbox.publish(order);
  return order;
}
```

